00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:18.160
Federated, Not Centralized. Data doesn't move to a central database.

198
00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:23.740
It stays where it is. Everyone keeps their toys. Epic systems keep their data.

199
00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:32.420
Apple Health keeps its data. Cerner keeps its data. Your framework's protocol just lets you run queries ACROSS systems without

200
00:21:32.420 --> 00:21:38.640
moving data. Like asking every library in the world a question without stealing their books.

201
00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:43.600
Federated data networks already do this with three hundred million plus patient records.

202
00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:49.040
This solves: General Data Protection Regulation, or G.D.P.R.

203
00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:53.840
and Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, or H.I.P.A.A.

204
00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:59.900
compliance (data never leaves source). Privacy concerns (no central honeypot to hack).

205
00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:06.720
Vendor cooperation (they keep control). Patient trust (data doesn't go to "the cloud").

206
00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:10.900
Why This Works: The Mathematical Impossibility of Committees.

207
00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:17.800
About two hundred F.D.A. bureaucrats decide what eight billion people can try when dying.

208
00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:25.120
Here's the math on why that can't work: The F.D.A.'s processing capacity: two hundred committee members, working eight hours

209
00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:30.620
a day, making one decision per hour equals one thousand six hundred decisions per day.

210
00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:39.160
The decisions that actually need making: ten thousand known diseases times one hundred potential treatments times eight

211
00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:45.040
hundred million genetically distinct patient profiles equals eight hundred billion decisions.

212
00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:53.080
At one thousand six hundred decisions per day, the F.D.A. would need one million three hundred sixty-nine thousand eight

213
00:22:53.080 --> 00:23:01.600
hundred sixty-three years to evaluate everything. A decentralized system where eight billion people each make one health

214
00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:05.960
decision per day processes the same load in one hundred days.

215
00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:10.940
The F.D.A. doesn't know: What disease YOU have (they haven't met you).

216
00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:14.640
How YOUR body responds to treatments (genetics are unique).

217
00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:19.740
What risks YOU'RE willing to take (some prefer death to side effects, others the reverse).

218
00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:24.730
Whether YOU'D rather die trying or die waiting (only you can answer this).

219
00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:29.900
But they decide for you anyway. This isn't ideology. It's information theory.

220
00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:35.640
You mathematically cannot centralize medical decisions for eight billion unique people.

221
00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:43.420
The Future: Where Death Becomes Embarrassing. Twenty twenty-seven: The Beginning of the End of Dying Slowly.

222
00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:49.340
F.D.A. dot gov becomes actually useful. Millions join trials from home.

223
00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:55.120
The first "Wikipedia disease" gets cured entirely through crowdsourced trials.

224
00:23:55.180 --> 00:24:00.160
The F.D.A. claims credit. Twenty thirty: Big Pharma Pivots or Dies.

225
00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:08.240
Pharmaceutical companies realize they can't charge ten thousand dollars for pills that cost one dollar to make when everyone

226
00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:15.240
can see the data. Some adapt. Some become museums. The gift shop sells expired painkillers at original markup.

227
00:24:16.100 --> 00:24:21.420
Twenty thirty-five: The Great Revelation. Humanity could have done this all along.

228
00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:26.380
The internet existed since nineteen ninety. Computers since nineteen fifty.

229
00:24:27.460 --> 00:24:31.220
Five thousand years of letting people die while filling out forms.

230
00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:35.680
History books will call this "The Paperwork Age." Children will laugh.

231
00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:41.140
Twenty fifty: Death Becomes Opt-In. Diseases are mostly solved.

232
00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:48.880
Death becomes a choice, like smoking or voting Republican. The F.D.A.'s job becomes preventing people from becoming immortal

233
00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:53.880
too quickly (traffic is bad enough). The last F.D.A. form is filled out.

234
00:24:54.320 --> 00:25:02.020
It's immediately lost. Nobody notices. How Your D.F.D.A. (the decentralized F.D.A.) Actually Works.

235
00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:10.800
This approach achieves forty-four point one times More Efficiency through: Cost per patient is nine hundred twenty-nine

236
00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:19.190
dollars versus the current forty-one thousand dollars. Time to results is 2 years versus the current 17 years.

237
00:25:19.720 --> 00:25:25.520
Patient access is universal access versus eighty-six point one percent excluded currently.
